CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf83-00000004:None
CREATED:20260824T131252Z
DESCRIPTION:In this concert\, two of J. S. Bach's most intimate solo canta
 tas-"Ich bin vergnügt mit meinem Glücke\," BWV 84\, and "Vergnügte Ruh\, 
 beliebte Seelenlust\," BWV 170-explore themes of contentment\, contemplat
 ion\, and spiritual reflection. Selections from Handel's exquisite Italia
 n chamber duets\, later transformed into "Messiah\," offer a fascinating 
 glimpse into the composer's creative process and the journey from private
  music-making to public masterpiece.\n\nFree admission. Paid parking is a
 vailable at the Bryan Center Parking Garage at 125 Science Dr. with ADA p
 arking in the surface lot at the same address.\n\n\nLearn more about the 
 composition and history of the music in a pre-concert talk by Chapel Orga
 nist Chad Fothergill at 4:00 p.m. in the Divinity School's Goodson Chapel
 .\n\nFamilies with young children\, and those not able to attend the conc
 ert\, are invited to drop in on a dress rehearsal on Saturday\, September
  26\, from 1:30 to 4:00 p.m. in Duke Chapel.\n\nBach's cantatas stand amo
 ng the great achievements of sacred music\, uniting theological depth\, d
 ramatic immediacy\, and extraordinary musical invention. The 2026-2027 se
 ries\, "Odes & Oratorios\," explores music ranging from intimate devotion
  to public celebration\, from chamber-scale reflection to works of festiv
 e grandeur.
DURATION:PT1H15M
