CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20260915T173404Z
DESCRIPTION:The pace of production for new and existing synthetic chemical
 s is continually increasing\, rapidly outpacing our capacity to assess ri
 sks of exposure to these substances. Studies of the chemical exposome (ch
 emicals accumulated in the body over a lifetime of exposure) have reporte
 d the presence of thousands of synthetic substances in human blood and th
 ese exposures likely contribute a significant portion of chronic disease 
 risk in the general population\; however\, we lack critical information o
 n where these chemicals come from\, how they accumulate in the body\, and
  implications for human health. Here I will discuss my research group's w
 ork towards answering the question: How does molecular structure impact a
  chemical's potential to accumulate in the body\, and how can we use this
  knowledge to prioritize chemicals of concern during nontarget analysis (
 NTA)? I will primarily focus on per/polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFASs) as
  a representative chemical class. Information gleaned from measuring rela
 tive affinities of novel PFASs can be used to interpret results of human 
 biomonitoring through the lens of bioaccumulation potential\, offering in
 sights on potential exposure sources and novel PFAS toxicokinetics.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260915T173404Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260928T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T173404Z
