 ana\, Kayla
X-BEDEWORK-COST:Free!
CREATED:20260826T161301Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us on Monday\, September 28th at 5:00 PM in East Duke 209
  for a public lecture by Dr. Hortense Spillers\, a black feminist thinker
  of unparalleled influence and a foundational figure in African American 
 literary and cultural theory. Reception to follow. RSVP required (please 
 click the "More event information" button to access the RSVP form). \n\nT
 alk title: Oedipal Deferral: Implications of the Middle Passage\n\nHorten
 se J. Spillers is Gertrude Conaway Vanderbilt Professor of English\, Emer
 ita\, at Vanderbilt University. She received her Ph.D. from Brandeis Univ
 ersity and has held appointments at Wellesley College\, Haverford College
 \, Emory University\, and Cornell University. Her visiting positions incl
 ude Duke University's Program in Literature and the John F. Kennedy Insti
 tute for North American Studies at the Free University of Berlin. Spiller
 s' scholarship spans American and African American literature\, Black fem
 inist theory\, psychoanalysis\, and cultural criticism. Her influential e
 ssay\, "Mama's Baby\, Papa's Maybe: An American Grammar Book\," is widely
  cited across disciplines and continues to shape the study of race\, gend
 er\, and power. She is the author of Black\, White\, and In Color: Essays
  on American Literature and Culture and co-editor of Conjuring: Black Wom
 en\, Fiction\, and Literary Tradition. She served as a period editor for 
 The Norton Anthology of African American Literature and edited Comparativ
 e American Identities for the English Institute series. She has held fell
 owships at the National Humanities Center and the Center for Advanced Stu
 dy in the Behavioral Sciences at Stanford\, with additional support from 
 the Rockefeller and Ford Foundations. A member of the boundary 2 editoria
 l collective\, she has also served on the Executive Council of the Modern
  Language Association. Among her many honors are the Callaloo Lifetime Ac
 hievement Award\, the Nicolás Guillén Lifetime Achievement Award from the
  Caribbean Philosophical Association\, induction into the American Academ
 y of Arts and Sciences (2022)\, and an honorary doctorate from Yale Unive
 rsity (2024). She resides in Nashville\, Tennessee.
DURATION:PT1H30M
